Q: How can I make a recursive function return an array of a given number set to zero? A: <p>You can omit second parameter entirely and correct recursion base to return empty array in case <code>n = 0</code>.</p>
<p>Please, take a loak at below code:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function rangeUp(n) {
    if (n &lt; 0) return console.error("The number is invalid.");
    if (n == 0) return [0] // recursion base
    return [...rangeUp(n - 1), n];
}

console.log(rangeUp(5))</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Variadic parameter and additional default value not compiling A: <p>You can have a look at <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/66402319/2229039">this post</a> that deals with a similar topic, i.e. how to deal with <code>std::source_location</code> and variadic parameters.</p>
<p>In your context, you would have:</p>
<pre><code>struct FormatWithLocation {
  const char* value;
  std::source_location loc;

  FormatWithLocation(const char* s,
         const std::source_location&amp; l = std::source_location::current())
      : value(s), loc(l) {}
};

template&lt;class X&gt;
struct LOGGER {
    template&lt;class ...LogArguments&gt;
    auto _LOG(FormatWithLocation fmt, LogArguments... logArguments) -&gt; void
    {
        char formattedLog [BUFFER_SIZE] {};
        snprintf( formattedLog, sizeof(formattedLog), fmt.value, logArguments... );
        LogControllerContainer::dispatchLog( static_cast&lt;X*&gt;(this)-&gt;m_member, formattedLog, fmt.loc);
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/9Y78d5vKv" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p><strong>However</strong>, this will not solve the issue with <code>_LOG(&quot;Foo has %d&quot;);</code>. You could have a look at <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/format/format.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">std::format</a>; you would follow a more c++-ish way (rather than C) to solve it.</p>
